# The ledger of `json/jq`: what the jq libraries of the three languages let a
# program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. None of the
# three standard libraries runs jq, so the yardstick is the author's of
# 2026-11-17: jq itself (C) as the reference, gojq (Go, github.com/itchyny/gojq
# v0.12.20), jaq (Rust: the crates jaq-core, jaq-std and jaq-json) and
# Python's `jq` bindings 1.13.0. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17:
# `go doc -all` of the gojq module fetched into the scratch folder, the item
# lists docs.rs prints for the three jaq crates, and `dir()` of the Python
# module installed there. The language is jq 1.7's, its manual of the tag
# jq-1.7.1; the installed jq answers 1.8.2, and where 1.7.1 and 1.8.2 differ the
# note says which was taken. The tests are jq 1.7.1's own, as this ledger of
# 2026-11-18 took them from its `tests/jq.test`.

capability jq/parse  a filter read and checked once, to be run any number of times
  heroes  json/jq.parse, json/jq.is_valid, json/jq.Program, json/jq.MAX_NESTING
  go      Parse, Compile, Query, Code, ParseError, ParseError.Error, CompilerOption
  rust    load::parse, load::lex::Lexer, load::lex::Token, load::lex::Tok, load::lex::StrPart, load::lex::Expect, load::parse::Parser, load::parse::Expect, load::parse::Error, load::parse::Result, load::parse::TError, load::Error, load::Errors, load::span, compile::Compiler, compile::Filter, compile::Undefined, compile::Errors, compile::Error, Compiler, Filter, defs, funs
  python  jq.compile, _Program, _Program.program_string
  note    a filter is read with jq's builtins written in jq before it, every name resolved once, so a misspelt function is refused by `parse` and not when the run reaches it; a `$name` no binding names is read from `Options.variables` when the run reaches it, since the variables are given per run (gojq asks for their names at compile time). The reader nests at most `MAX_NESTING` levels

capability jq/run  every value a filter outputs for one input
  heroes  json/jq.run, json/jq.run_with, json/jq.Outcome
  go      Query.Run, Code.Run, Iter, Iter.Next, NewIter
  rust    Filter::run, Ctx, Ctx::new, native::run, box_iter::BoxIter, box_iter::Results
  python  _Program.input_value, _Program.input, _ProgramWithInput.all, _ProgramWithInput.iter, jq.all, jq.iter
  note    every value, in order; the values output before an error are kept in `Outcome.outputs`, as jq prints them before the error

capability jq/run-first  the first value a filter outputs, the run stopped there
  heroes  json/jq.first
  go      Iter.Next (called once)
  rust    Iterator::next on the outputs
  python  _ProgramWithInput.first, jq.first
  note    the run stops at the first value: `first(range(infinite))` ends

capability jq/run-text  a filter run over every value of a JSON text, as jq run on a file
  heroes  json/jq.run_text
  go      -
  rust    jaq_json::read::parse_many, jaq_json::read::read_many, jaq_json::read::parse_single, jaq_json::read::Error
  python  _Program.input_text, _Program.input_values, _ProgramWithInput.text, jq.text
  note    values one after another, white space between them or none, each the input of its own run and the rest given to `input`, as jq run on a file does

capability jq/variables  variables the caller gives, read as `$name`, as `--arg` and `--argjson` give them
  heroes  json/jq.Options (its `variables`)
  go      WithVariables, Code.Run (its `values`)
  rust    Vars, Ctx::new (its variables)
  python  jq.compile (its `args`)
  note    `$name` for each, and `$__named` and `$ARGS.named` for them all, as jq's `--arg` and `--argjson` give them

capability jq/inputs  the values `input` and `inputs` read
  heroes  json/jq.Options (its `inputs`), json/jq.Outcome (its `read`)
  go      WithInputIter
  rust    jaq_std::input::HasInputs, jaq_std::input::Inputs, jaq_std::input::RcIter, jaq_std::input::funs
  python  _Program.input_values (each value after the first)
  note    what `input` read is counted, so a caller running one filter over a stream skips it, as jq does

capability jq/environment  what `$ENV` and `env` read
  heroes  json/jq.Options (its `environment`)
  go      WithEnvironLoader
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the caller's object, empty by default: a filter from outside never reads the process's environment unless the program puts it there; the example program does

capability jq/bounds  a run bounded in steps, outputs and depth, so a filter cannot hang or fill memory
  heroes  json/jq.Options (its `max_steps`, `max_outputs` and `max_depth`), json/jq.LIMIT, json/jq.DEFAULT_MAX_STEPS, json/jq.DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUTS, json/jq.DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH
  go      Code.RunWithContext, Query.RunWithContext (cancellation by a context)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    none of the three bounds a run by itself; here every run is bounded by default. A step is one move of the machine; a builtin that copies or walks a value counts a step an element or 16 bytes, its cost counted before it allocates, and no builtin makes more than 4,000,000 elements of one value (`[., .]` built forty times over shares its parts and holds more than any memory). Measured on 2026-11-17: the hostile filters tried, endless recursion, `[range(1e9)]`, a text doubled a hundred times, a value shared 2^40 times over written, compared, flattened and walked, all end `limited`, the largest at 645 MB, `[paths]` over the shared value

capability jq/errors  how a run ended: the value an error carried, the message jq prints, a halt and its exit code
  heroes  json/jq.Ending, json/jq.INVALID
  go      ValueError, HaltError, HaltError.ExitCode, HaltError.Value, HaltError.Error
  rust    Error, Exn, val::ValR, val::ValX, val::ValRs, val::ValXs, val::unwrap_valr
  python  ValueError (raised by the run)
  note    `raised` keeps the error's value and the message jq prints for it, `halted` the exit code and the value of `halt_error`; jq 1.7.1's messages, which its test file holds, where 1.8.2 adds the value

capability jq/messages  what `debug` and `stderr` wrote, kept for the caller
  heroes  json/jq.Outcome (its `messages`)
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `debug` writes `["DEBUG:", v]` and `stderr` the value, kept for the caller instead of a standard error a library does not own

capability jq/format  a value written as jq writes it, on one line or indented
  heroes  json/jq.format_value, json/jq.format_value_indented
  go      Marshal, Preview
  rust    jaq_json::write::write, jaq_json::write::Pp, jaq_json::write::Styles, jaq_json::format_val, jaq_json::style, jaq_json::write_byte, jaq_json::write_bytes, jaq_json::write_seq, jaq_json::write_utf8, jaq_json::write_val
  python  _ProgramWithInput.text
  note    a double as jq writes one: the fewest digits, an exponent where the point stands 4 or more places before the digits or 15 past them, `nan` as `null` and an infinity as the largest double; `json.format` writes JavaScript's form, which differs at `1e+17`

capability jq/order  two values ordered as jq orders them
  heroes  json/jq.compare
  go      Compare
  rust    ops::Cmp, jaq_json::Val (its `Ord`)
  python  -
  note    jq's order of types and values, `nan` before every number, objects by sorted keys then values; held on a stack, and within a budget inside a run

capability jq/type  the name jq gives a value's type
  heroes  json/jq.type_of
  go      TypeOf
  rust    val::ValT
  python  -
  note    -

capability jq/numbers-exact  numbers past an `f64`: big integers and a literal's own digits
  heroes  pending
  go      ToInt, ToFloat64 (and `*big.Int` values)
  rust    jaq_json::Num
  python  -
  note    a `json.Value` holds every number as an `f64`; jq 1.7 keeps a literal's own digits (`100000000000000000001`, `1.000`) and gojq big integers. It needs a number case of `json.Value` that keeps its text, which is `json`'s to give

capability jq/custom-functions  a function of the program's own called from a filter
  heroes  pending
  go      WithFunction, WithIterFunction
  rust    Native, native::bome, native::unary, native::v, native::Fun, native::Filter, data::DataT, data::HasLut, data::JustLut
  python  -
  note    a function of the program's own named before `parse`, so that a misspelt name is still refused there; not done in time

capability jq/modules  `import`, `include`, `module` and `modulemeta`: definitions read from other files
  heroes  refused: `import`, `include` and `modulemeta` read files by a search path, so a filter from outside would make a library touch the disk; definitions are written in the filter's own text, which `def` reads
  go      WithModuleLoader, NewModuleLoader, ModuleLoader, Import
  rust    load::Loader, load::Module, load::Modules, load::Import, load::File, load::Arena, load::import
  python  -
  note    `parse` refuses the three with a message saying so; jq.test's module cases are tests of that refusal

capability jq/syntax-tree  the filter's syntax tree as public values, printed back as text
  heroes  refused: the tree is the interpreter's own; public types for it would fix the reader's shape for every program, and the filter's text is the one public form of it
  go      Query.String, Term, TermType, Index, Func, FuncDef, If, IfElif, Try, Reduce, Foreach, Label, Object, ObjectKeyVal, ConstTerm, ConstArray, ConstObject, ConstObjectKeyVal, ConstObject.ToValue, Array, String, Pattern, PatternObject, Suffix, Unary, Operator, Operator.String, Operator.GoString, TermType.GoString (and each type's `String`)
  rust    load::parse::Term, load::parse::Pattern, load::parse::BinaryOp, load::parse::Def, compile::TermId, compile::Lut, Lut, Cv, PathsPtr, RunPtr, UpdatePtr
  python  -
  note    gojq and jaq give theirs, each its own and not the other's

capability jq/language-paths  `.`, `.foo`, `.[i]`, `.[a:b]`, `.[]`, `?`, `..`, and `path`, `paths`, `getpath`, `setpath`, `delpaths`, `del`, `pick`, `to_entries`, `from_entries`, `with_entries`, `leaf_paths`
  heroes  json/jq.parse (the paths, `?`, `..`), json/jq.run (`path`, `paths`, `getpath`, `setpath`, `delpaths`, `del`, `pick`, the entries, `leaf_paths`)
  go      (the language)
  rust    path::Path, path::Part, path::Opt
  python  (the language)
  note    `path(f)` follows paths through pipes, `,`, `if`, `//`, `select`, `recurse`, `getpath`, `first` and `limit`, and refuses a value made inside it with jq's messages; `delpaths` removes every path at once, negative indices counted from the end first, as jq does

capability jq/language-core  pipes, `,`, parentheses, literals, array and object construction, string interpolation, `if`, `and`, `or`, `not`, `//`, `try` and `catch`, `error`, `reduce`, `foreach`, `label` and `break`, `as` with destructuring and `?//`, `def` with arguments, recursion
  heroes  json/jq.parse, json/jq.run
  go      (the language)
  rust    ops::Math, Bind, val::Range
  python  (the language)
  note    the orders of jq's generators kept: an operator's right side the outer loop, an object's first key the outer loop. Errors on the left of `//` go on, as jq 1.8.2 answers (1.7.1's was not observed here). `repeat(f)` repeats `f` on its own input, as jq 1.7.1's manual and jq 1.8.2 answer. A filter's own nesting is bounded by `MAX_NESTING`; a chain of pipes, commas or definitions is not

capability jq/language-operators  `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` on every type, as jq defines them
  heroes  json/jq.parse, json/jq.run
  go      (the language)
  rust    ops::Math, ops::Cmp
  python  (the language)
  note    jq 1.7.1's: a text times a negative number is `null`, times a number under 1 the empty text; `%` on the integers of two numbers, a `nan` giving `nan`, and `(remainder)` in its message

capability jq/language-assignment  `=`, `|=`, `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`, `//=`
  heroes  json/jq.parse, json/jq.run
  go      (the language)
  rust    (the language)
  python  (the language)
  note    `a |= f` takes `f`'s first value and removes the path where there is none, the removals made at once at the end, as jq 1.7.1 does; places none of which holds another are set in one pass that copies each container once, so `.[] |= f` on 100,000 elements takes one pass (measured 2026-11-17), and other places one after another

capability jq/builtins-collections  length, keys, has, in, inside, contains, map, map_values, add, any, all, flatten, range, sort, sort_by, group_by, unique, unique_by, min, max, min_by, max_by, reverse, indices, index, rindex, combinations, transpose, bsearch, walk, first, last, nth, limit, until, while, repeat, recurse, isempty, select, empty, the type filters, IN, INDEX, JOIN, tostream, fromstream, truncate_stream, splits, getpath, env, builtins, input, inputs, debug, stderr, halt, halt_error, $__loc__
  heroes  json/jq.run (every one named)
  go      (the language)
  rust    jaq_std::funs, jaq_std::defs, jaq_std::base_funs, jaq_std::extra_funs, jaq_json::funs, jaq_json::defs
  python  (the language)
  note    `limit`, `first`, `isempty`, ranges and `inputs` run lazily, so `first(range(infinite))` ends; `nth(n; f)` gives nothing past the end and refuses a negative `n`, as 1.7.1's tests ask; `flatten` reads an object's values; `getpath` and `paths` as jq's

capability jq/builtins-text  utf8bytelength, startswith, endswith, ltrimstr, rtrimstr, trim, ltrim, rtrim, explode, implode, split, join, ascii_downcase, ascii_upcase, tostring, tonumber, tojson, fromjson, @text, @json
  heroes  json/jq.run (every one named)
  go      (the language)
  rust    jaq_json::bstr, jaq_json::bytes_valrs
  python  (the language)
  note    jq 1.7.1's: `ltrimstr` and `rtrimstr` give their input back where it or the argument is not a text (1.8.2 refuses it); `tonumber` reads its text as a JSON number, white space around it allowed, where jq 1.8.2 refuses it; `indices` and the slices count code points, as jq 1.8.2 answers

capability jq/builtins-regex  test, match, capture, scan, split/2, splits, sub, gsub, with jq's flags
  heroes  json/jq.run (every one named)
  go      (the language)
  rust    (the language)
  python  (the language)
  note    through `regex`, in time linear in the text: Perl's rule for the match taken, so Oniguruma's flag `l` (longest) is refused, as are back-references and look-around; offsets in code points; `sub` and `gsub` with a replacement of several values give one result for each, as jq 1.8.2 answers

capability jq/builtins-formats  @html, @uri, @csv, @tsv, @sh, @base64, @base64d, @base32, @base32d, alone and before a string
  heroes  json/jq.run (every one named)
  go      (the language)
  rust    (the language)
  python  (the language)
  note    `@base64d` writes bytes that are not UTF-8 as U+FFFD, one for each byte that begins no character, where jq 1.8.2 writes fewer on one case found

capability jq/builtins-math  floor, sqrt, pow, log and the rest of C's mathematics that jq names, infinite, nan, isinfinite, isnan, isnormal, the number filters
  heroes  json/jq.run (every one named but the Bessel functions)
  go      (the language)
  rust    (the language)
  python  (the language)
  note    through `math`; `frexp`, `modf` and `lgamma_r` give lists, as jq's

capability jq/builtins-dates  gmtime, mktime, strftime, strptime, todate, fromdate, dateadd, now, localtime, strflocaltime
  heroes  json/jq.run (gmtime, mktime, strftime, strptime, todate, fromdate, todateiso8601, fromdateiso8601, dateadd, datesub, date, now, localtime, strflocaltime)
  go      (the language)
  rust    (the language)
  python  (the language)
  note    the proleptic Gregorian calendar computed with no table; the local forms read the zone as UTC and `now` reads `Options.now`, since a filter here has no clock and no zone of its own

capability jq/builtins-bessel  the Bessel functions jq names: j0, j1, y0, y1, jn, yn
  heroes  pending
  go      (the language)
  rust    -
  python  (the language)
  note    `math` holds none; written here they would be a second copy of what belongs there

dropped
  go      Code.Run's variadic `values` past WithVariables' names (the same capability as jq/variables), gojq's `ParseError.Offset` and `Token` (the message says the line), ToFloat64 and ToInt (helpers of a `WithFunction` body)
  rust    box_iter::box_once, box_iter::flat_map_then, box_iter::flat_map_then_with, box_iter::flat_map_with, box_iter::map_with, box_iter::then (iterator plumbing), jaq_json::Rc and jaq_json::Map (the value's own storage), jaq_json::Error, jaq_json::ValR, jaq_json::ValX (a run's answer, jq/errors)
  python  jq.jq and _Program.transform (deprecated forms of compile and run), _EmptyValue, _ErrorStore, _JqStatePool, _NO_VALUE, _ResultIterator, _compilation_lock, _iter, io, json, threading (the module's private names and its imports)
